1
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:32,250
Making fun and games is anything but fun and games.

2
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:58,500
Indie Game: The Movie is a documentary film directed by Lisanne Pajot.

3
00:19:18,750 --> 00:19:24,750
Follows the dramatic journeys of video game developers as they
create and release their games to the world.

4
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:51,000
It's about making video games, but at its core, it's about the
creative process, and exposing yourself through your work.

5
00:32:11,250 --> 00:32:17,250
Self, played by Edmund McMillen.

6
00:38:37,500 --> 00:38:43,500
Self, played by Tommy Refenes.

7
00:45:03,750 --> 00:45:09,750
Self, played by Phil Fish.

8
00:51:30,000 --> 00:51:36,000
Self, played by Jonathan Blow.

9
00:57:56,250 --> 00:58:02,250
Self, played by Anthony Carboni.

10
01:04:22,500 --> 01:04:28,500
Indie Game: The Movie is a 2012 documentary film made by
Canadian filmmakers James Swirsky and Lisanne Pajot.

11
01:10:48,750 --> 01:10:54,750
The film is about the struggles of independent game developers Edmund McMillen and Tommy Refenes during the development of Super
Meat Boy, Phil Fish during the development of Fez, and also Jonathan Blow, who reflects on the success of Braid.

12
01:17:15,000 --> 01:17:21,000
After two successful Kickstarter funds, interviews were conducted
with prominent indie developers within the community.

13
01:23:41,250 --> 01:23:47,250
After recording over 300 hours of footage, Swirsky and Pajot decided to
cut the movie down to follow the four developers selected.

14
01:30:07,500 --> 01:30:13,500
Their reasoning behind this was to show game development in the "past,
present and future" tenses through each individual's story.

15
01:36:33,750 --> 01:36:39,750
The film explores themes of game, woman director,
computer programming, programmer.
